00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.300
So every time we seem
to come on the podcast,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.490
we talk about GPUs
getting cheaper.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.770
So why don't we talk about
GPUs getting cheaper?

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.080
So we're introducing preemtible
GPUs, which is kind of neat.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:32.040
So if you ever used
preemtible GCE instances--

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.590
so instances that may
die in about 24 hours,

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.520
but you get a very large
deduction in price when you use

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:39.280
them--

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.430
you're now able to use GPU
instances on those, now in beta

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.542
and available for GPUs
on preemtible instances.

00:02:45.542 --> 00:02:47.000
We'll have a link
in the show notes

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.200
as well to the blog post, which
links to the documentation

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.199
and how you can do them.

00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:53.464
But basically, if you want to do
batch type processing for GPUs,

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:55.380
and you want it to be a
lot cheaper to do it--

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.600
especially if you're using
preemtible instances already--

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.920
this is a really
nice thing for you.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.370
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.780
Cheaper is always better.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.530
