00:04:48.771 --> 00:04:53.520
So we are excited that we have
with us Malika Cantor, who

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.950
is the global lead
of Launchpad Studio,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.670
and Peter Norvig, who is the
research director of machine

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.240
learning at Google.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.594
So before we get started and to
talking about Launchpad Studio,

00:05:04.594 --> 00:05:06.760
we want to talk a little
bit about your backgrounds.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.790
So please introduce
yourself a little more.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:09.623
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah.

00:05:09.623 --> 00:05:11.280
So my name is Malika Cantor.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.050
As you mentioned, I'm the
global lead at Launchpad Studio.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:19.080
Launchpad sits under
developer relations at Google.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.150
We are basically the
accelerator engine of Google,

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.930
so we run different
accelerator initiatives

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.930
focused on supporting
various startup

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:28.680
ecosystems across the world.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.260
And Studio is focused on
supporting specifically applied

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.480
AI and machine learning
companies globally.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.540
MELANIE WARRICK: Thanks.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.373
And Peter, we don't
really know who you are,

00:05:36.373 --> 00:05:38.862
so do you mind telling us
a little about yourself?

00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:39.570
PETER NORVIG: Hi.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.069
I'm Peter.

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:42.940
Norvig I'm a director of
research here at Google.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.360
I've been at Google since 2001.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.640
Company was a little
bit smaller then.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.970
And I've been
focusing on machine

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.620
learning for a long time,
working on various tools

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.210
and applications.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.870
And about a year ago, I started
getting involved with Launchpad

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.380
to help others learn
how to use those tools.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.500
MELANIE WARRICK: And you did
write the canonical book on AI

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.095
that most people tend to look
back to with Stuart Russell.

00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:11.220
PETER NORVIG: That's right.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:15.030
So I'm in the dot
com domain now,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.500
but I've had all
the top levels--

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.770
as in dot edu, and was
professor for a while,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:23.970
I was in dot gov in
computer science at NASA--

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:27.110
and along the way,
wrote a textbook

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:29.370
and got involved in
writing about the project.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.820
MELANIE WARRICK: Got a little
bit of background there.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.236
I know, Malika, we were talking
about it too-- that you've

00:06:33.236 --> 00:06:36.029
had some great experiences
around the VC space,

00:06:36.029 --> 00:06:38.070
especially in China, and
we want to get into that

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.070
a little further later.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.420
But first, let's talk a little
bit about Launchpad Studio.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.540
So you're explaining
an overview of it,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.940
but what led to this project?

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.167
What inspired you guys
to get going with it?

00:06:50.167 --> 00:06:51.000
MALIKA CANTOR: Sure.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.500
So as you mentioned, I was
working in venture capital,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.630
investing in applied
AI, machine learning,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.760
and robotics companies
for a little while.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.270
And one of the things I realized
was that a lot of the startups

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.070
didn't really have
the tools that they

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.060
needed to be successful.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.630
And so one of my thoughts
was, well, where can

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.750
I go, and what can I do to
actually make a difference?

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.970
And this was about
six months ago.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.760
I started, like Peter, getting
involved with the Launchpad

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.180
team and saw a great opportunity
to build a program that

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:24.270
would actually allow us to
gather more insights on what

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:27.000
startups working on
applied machine learning

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.770
in different industries
actually needed,

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.260
and for us to then
go back to Google

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.690
and other potential
large tech companies

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.150
and inform them on what were
the tools that these startups

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.640
needed to actually
implement things at scale

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.230
and grow and be successful?

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:46.050
So that was sort of why I
got involved very early on.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:51.060
We started talking with Peter
about his interest in, again,

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.800
gathering these insights--

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.290
almost like
developing a lab where

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.224
we were working with
great companies,

00:07:58.224 --> 00:07:59.640
trying different
things with them,

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.610
bringing them amazing
mentors, and then

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.550
figuring out if there
were any sort of learnings

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.820
across these different companies
and industries that would then

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.550
indicate that there was
an opportunity to build

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.780
a tool on the Google side.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.360
And so this could be an API.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:17.490
This could be a
new type of service

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:21.630
that would then have impacts
across the startup ecosystem

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.832
and everywhere in the world
and across industries.

00:08:23.832 --> 00:08:25.540
MELANIE WARRICK: And
the program itself--

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:29.460
it's a six month incubator
program to bring in startups,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.060
is that right?

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.393
MALIKA CANTOR: Effectively, yes.

00:08:31.393 --> 00:08:34.000
So it's a six month program.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.700
So once we decided
that we basically

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.378
wanted to develop all
of these insights,

00:08:37.378 --> 00:08:39.419
we started thinking sort
of from first principles

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.429
how it would make
sense to set it up.

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:44.670
And we decided together to
focus on one specific industry

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.190
vertical at a time to
allow us to really go

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.830
deep and understand
what was going on there

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.102
and gather really
solid insights.

00:08:52.102 --> 00:08:54.060
So right now, we're
focused on the applications

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:57.270
of AI, machine learning,
and health care and biotech.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.180
So we basically
onboard companies

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.362
on a rolling basis that are
focused on this vertical.

00:09:03.362 --> 00:09:05.070
We already have four
companies that we've

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.810
been working with for
a couple of months

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.402
and checking in with them
every couple of weeks.

00:09:10.402 --> 00:09:12.360
They come in and work
with us every few months,

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.276
and we'll be onboarding
new companies actually

00:09:14.276 --> 00:09:16.080
just in two weeks.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.940
We have a big Next Event.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.049
And so they come in,
they meet with a lot

00:09:21.049 --> 00:09:22.590
of different product
teams at Google,

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:24.319
but also mentors outside.

00:09:24.319 --> 00:09:25.860
And then in this
whole process, we're

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.900
basically gathering information
about some of their challenges.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.460
So for instance, I've
been using TensorFlow,

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.850
but I just can't figure
out how to scale up

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.830
this part of my infrastructure.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:42.390
Or I have this data set, but
especially in the health care

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.480
space, the disease I'm
looking at only allows

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.340
for me to have quite
a limited data set.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.670
How can I amplify and augment
that data set to actually use

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.830
some of the models that are best
used in the machine learning

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:54.330
space?

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:55.740
A lot of these
types of questions

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.810
we're already starting
to see, and I'd

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.210
love to talk more about some
of the other overarching

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.970
challenges that we're
already starting to identify.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.772
MARK MANDEL: So you're
actually talking about that.

00:10:05.772 --> 00:10:07.980
In the beginning, you said
there are these tools that

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.540
are missing as though
it was the tools that

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:10.950
are missing for those
sort of problems

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:11.850
that people are trying to solve.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:12.930
Is that kind of the gist?

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.471
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah, and I might
also let Peter jump in here.

00:10:15.471 --> 00:10:18.270
I think all of this is
very much a hypothesis.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.600
We sort of came at this-- and
maybe a good analogy is we

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.210
were thinking, all right,
lean startup methodologies.

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:28.010
We've been accustomed
to hearing this term,

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.220
and we are thinking about
it with machine learning.

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:33.030
And this is something I started
seeing already in my VC days--

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.770
is does it really work?

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.180
Can you fail fast?

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.370
Can you pivot as
a company that's

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.230
leveraging machine learning?

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:43.290
And our general sort
of understanding

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:44.520
was, well, no actually.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.740
We don't really think
that it's possible,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.930
and it's because the
tools are lacking.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.040
There are some tools--

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.430
like TensorFlow is an
incredible tool, GCP--

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.300
we have a lot of
other ones out there,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.470
and other people are
building great tools too.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.840
But it's still extremely
difficult as a company

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.490
leveraging these
new technologies

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:05.910
to do what we think startups
should be able to do.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:09.360
And so through Studio, our hope
is to really try to understand

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.610
what are the limitations of
the tools that are already

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.950
on the market-- which are
quite limited, to be honest--

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:16.740
and then inform what are tools
that we should be building

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:20.700
to hopefully make it easier and
easier for startups to pivot

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:24.307
and to fail fast in the
machine learning space.

00:11:24.307 --> 00:11:26.640
PETER NORVIG: So I think it's
a combination of the tools

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.510
and the best practices and
the knowledge and experience.

00:11:31.510 --> 00:11:32.760
So I came to this.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.940
I had been working
internally with teams,

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:38.970
where a large
percentage of the team

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.790
have PhDs in machine
learning, right?

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:45.180
And so they had some
background in intuition

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:48.630
about what to build, but
it was still really hard.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.850
And now we want to say,
well, not everybody

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.150
has that luxury of
being able to have

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.830
a bunch of experts in machine
learning on your team.

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:59.040
Maybe you're in your domain--

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.490
whether it's health care,
energy, or transportation,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.530
or whatever your
company is doing--

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:07.220
and you mostly have
experts in that.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.050
And they don't have the
background that we had access

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.222
to, but you have an idea
that you have some data,

00:12:13.222 --> 00:12:15.180
you want to do something
useful with that data,

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.660
and you want to
produce a product.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:17.620
How can we do that?

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.950
And some of it is having the
right tool, but some of it

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.230
is just knowing what
the whole process is

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:25.560
of how you go about
starting from the raw data,

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.630
or even how do you go
about collecting data

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:31.920
if you don't have any yet and
building the whole pipeline

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.920
and evolving that
and keeping going?

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.430
And we just don't have
experience with that.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.680
We've done a lot of
one by ones, but if you

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.020
look at the history of
software engineering,

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.530
we've been figuring out how to
build software for 50 years.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.030
We've only been
trying to figure out

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.460
how to build machine
learning systems

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.370
for a small number of years.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:56.149
So understanding how all that
works is the key challenge now.

00:12:56.149 --> 00:12:58.440
MELANIE WARRICK: And you've
had the resources, finally,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.780
to be able to experiment
with building up this machine

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:01.279
learning.

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:03.780
But barriers, I know,
are definitely people.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.490
PETER NORVIG: Yeah,
so you see there

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.930
has been this big explosion
in the last couple of years,

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:11.910
and I think that's a couple
of things coming together.

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:14.430
A lot of it, as you say, has
to do with the resources--

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:18.370
that now more and
more data is online

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:21.120
as the internet is
everywhere, and data is

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.650
being captured in digital form.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:24.900
So that's available.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:26.425
Far greater computing power--

00:13:26.425 --> 00:13:28.800
that wasn't available in the
past to be able to do things

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.780
that you couldn't try before.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:33.420
And just I think one
success builds on another--

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.750
that if somebody proves
they can do something,

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.880
then somebody else says,
well, that worked for them.

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:40.130
I think I can do it now, too.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:41.880
MELANIE WARRICK: Out
of the four companies

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.470
that you're working
with currently,

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.850
what are some of the
tools that they are using

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.930
and then also some of the
tooling that you both think

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:49.680
could definitely--

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.350
I mean, we're talking on it--
but anything specific that's

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:54.937
standing out that you
think could be useful?

00:13:54.937 --> 00:13:55.770
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.230
So in the companies
right now-- again,

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.930
we're in the early
days of the program.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:04.440
A lot of it is mentorship--

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.740
giving insights in terms of
how they could be leveraging,

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.020
for instance, GCP
better, some of the APIs

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.730
that we've already
built. They're

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.250
working with teams like the
Cloud Machine Learning Engine

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.160
team, the Cloud AI
team, but then also

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:18.620
the Android Things
team, which is

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:22.089
trying to sort of push a lot of
learning onto the device now.

00:14:22.089 --> 00:14:24.630
We're basically working with a
lot of different product teams

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:26.730
that are coming in
and mentoring startups

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.617
on what are some of the
services that we have already

00:14:29.617 --> 00:14:31.950
that some of these companies
could be leveraging as part

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.980
of their stack so that they
don't build that themselves,

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:37.620
because there's no point there.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.220
What's interesting to the
software engineering point

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.279
that Peter was talking
about is a lot of the issues

00:14:44.279 --> 00:14:46.320
that we're seeing startups
are having is actually

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.780
around architecture.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.310
With software
development, you're

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:51.960
thinking about building
an end product.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:53.335
But with machine
learning, you're

00:14:53.335 --> 00:14:56.250
thinking about how to build
a scalable data pipeline

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.970
and how to retrain a model.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:02.310
And the end product is
almost like a byproduct.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:04.950
It's not necessarily what
you're moving towards.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.290
And so we're starting
to try to figure out

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.050
are there things that
we could be building?

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.540
And we definitely are on
the way to this already

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.150
with initiatives
like AutoML in terms

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:21.930
of automating certain parts
of this process of building

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:25.260
your software engineering
and development to help

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.740
these startups do this
more quickly and better--

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:32.280
the concept of potentially
automating DevOps also.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.636
I think there's a lot
of opportunities here.

00:15:34.636 --> 00:15:36.510
But I think that that
is one thing that we've

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:39.180
seen that's been really
interesting aside

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:44.610
from other problems like I
mentioned around data, UI/UX.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:47.850
To Peter's point,
there aren't tools

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.460
for understanding how
regulation is going

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.180
to affect your product,
and so there it

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.290
becomes more about
best practices

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:56.910
and sharing best
practices across

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:59.970
these different companies that
are working in one industry.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.520
Our hope is maybe that we
can even build a curriculum

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.140
to try to help inform--

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.450
OK, well, be aware
of regulation.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:09.330
Be aware of how to
go about building,

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:10.950
how to do software
development, how

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:12.510
to think about
your data pipeline,

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:13.990
how to think about UI and UX.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:15.930
And so, again, to
Peter's point, it

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.400
goes beyond just
the idea of tools.

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:20.910
It's more about sort of
sharing best practices

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:22.600
and learning from one another.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.240
MARK MANDEL: So that actually
sounds really interesting.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:25.200
I'm quite curious.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:27.570
When you're talking,
Peter, about the scarcity

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.880
of resources in terms of
people with the knowledge,

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:31.980
are you finding that one
of the big challenges

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.530
is that people who may come in
without that machine learning

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.870
or AI knowledge have
a more, I'm going

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:38.730
to say quote unquote,
"traditional" software

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.500
development background, so
the pipeline and the way

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.370
that they build things for
machine learning and AIs is--

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:46.440
I don't know how different--
but different enough

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:48.696
that it's causing a disconnect
or that's a big hurdle?

00:16:48.696 --> 00:16:50.070
Are you finding
that a challenge?

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.810
PETER NORVIG: Yeah,
I think that's right.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:55.260
So one of it is really
changing the mindset for what

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:56.550
you're trying to do, right?

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.420
In traditional
software engineering,

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.310
you're kind of thinking like
a mathematician or a logician.

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.869
And you say, well,
in the end point,

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:08.700
I could sort of prove
my program correct.

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.220
And in reality, you only
do that in the classroom.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:13.560
You don't do it for
any realistic program,

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.599
but you're sort of
aiming in that direction.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:20.220
I mean, we have tools
like test frameworks,

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.740
where you're making
assertions, and the assertions

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:27.250
are of the form the result of
this should be equal to that,

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.590
or this should be true.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:32.400
So it's very
binary, true/false--

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.750
either you got it or you don't.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.690
But with machine learning,
it's not like that.

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:38.800
It's not mathematical.

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:43.200
It's more like empirical
or scientific endeavor,

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.150
so it's like doing
biology, where

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.120
you do an experiment
on the world,

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:49.300
you observe the results
of the experiment.

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.950
It's not that it's
true or false,

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:53.640
it's just most of the time
it's going to be this,

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:56.140
and some of the time it's
going to be something else.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.650
And so making that
change from thinking

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:02.040
from a logic true
false to a kind

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:05.700
of probabilistic messy world--

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.680
that's hard for a lot
of people, to give up

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:11.040
on what they thought
was a solid bedrock

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.511
and now embrace
this uncertainty.

00:18:12.511 --> 00:18:14.760
MARK MANDEL: Kind of the
what do you mean this is only

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:15.980
going to happen sometimes?

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:16.510
PETER NORVIG: Yeah.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:18.760
MELANIE WARRICK: So one of
the things I wanted to know

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.120
is what do you think is making--

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.190
or what kind of companies do
you accept into the program?

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:26.250
What makes them successful
in this type of program?

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:29.610
MALIKA CANTOR: Yes, so
our selection criteria

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:30.700
is quite simple.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:34.680
So first, it's, are you
applying machine learning or AI

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:37.410
to the industry that
we've said we're

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.180
focused on at the present time?

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:41.092
Which is health care
and biotech right now,

00:18:41.092 --> 00:18:43.050
and we're going to be
announcing other industry

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:45.930
tracks in the course of 2018.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:49.410
The next one is are
you trying to solve

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.140
a specific challenge
related to machine learning

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.786
in the next six months?

00:18:53.786 --> 00:18:56.160
And the reason why that is
part of our selection criteria

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.730
is we don't want to
basically take startups

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:00.900
in the wrong direction.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:02.910
There are companies
that are going

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:05.580
to be implementing AI machine
learning at some point,

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:07.500
there are some companies
that never will.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.600
And we just want to make sure
that the companies that we do

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:12.180
work with-- because we put a
tremendous amount of resources

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.790
and time on our end,
but we also expect a lot

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.970
from the companies in terms
of being there in the program,

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:21.720
implementing a lot of the
feedback that we give them.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.690
We want it to be core
to what their roadmap is

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:25.920
in the next six months.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.530
One thing that I should
clarify is our main engagement

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:29.790
mechanism with the startups.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:32.430
So we don't actually
invest in the companies.

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:35.640
It's a slightly untraditional
accelerator program

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:39.150
in that we actually
get them to draw up

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:42.090
a project proposal of what
they want to accomplish

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.670
with Google in the
space of six months,

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.680
and we select them based
on their project proposal.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:51.810
So they actually have to give
us metrics, deliverables,

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.540
goals for what they
want to achieve,

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:57.060
and then we give them a lot
of feedback in the selection

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:57.610
process.

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.300
And then based on that
project, we actually

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.600
admit them into the program.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.290
MELANIE WARRICK:
What kind of metrics?

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:04.873
MALIKA CANTOR: It
really would depend.

00:20:04.873 --> 00:20:07.200
So if you're a computer
vision company looking

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.720
at cell dynamics,
and your project

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.140
is focused on improving
precision accuracy of detection

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.510
of a certain kind of disease
looking at those cells,

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:18.000
then it would be improving
your precision accuracy

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.400
from 83% to 96%.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.590
And that would be that
metric that we're basically

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:24.150
working on together.

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.940
But if it's a company working
in the natural language space,

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:29.320
then it might be
slightly different.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:36.120
It might be more about how many
errors can we detect in speech?

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.970
So it really depends from
one company to another.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:42.240
But yeah, that's, I'd say, is
the main selection criteria

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:43.140
for sure.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:48.690
For us, it's less about
selling Google products.

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:53.820
We don't really have a very
clear sort of direct gaining

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.582
back to Google, but Peter
and I definitely-- when we're

00:20:56.582 --> 00:20:58.290
looking at these
companies-- are thinking

00:20:58.290 --> 00:20:59.850
about what can we learn.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.890
And sometimes it's
even more just

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:05.070
from a personal intellectual
perspective of, wow,

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:07.050
this company is doing
something that we never

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.600
thought would be possible,
and we think there's something

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:11.520
really interesting
to learn here.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.790
But definitely we're,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:17.220
trying to figure out what
Google could be doing

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.890
and building to make this
whole ecosystem successful.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:23.090
So the more a company gives
us insights into that,

00:21:23.090 --> 00:21:24.840
the better it's going
to be, because we're

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:26.410
trying to do things
at scale here

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:29.730
beyond just making these four
companies and other companies

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:31.630
in the program successful too.

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.130
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
actually curious

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:36.090
almost at a higher level too.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.310
You obviously have
verticalized in the ML space,

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:39.960
but now I'm also curious.

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.000
Why specifically focusing on ML?

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.210
The startup space is huge.

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:45.270
We do a lot of stuff
across Google Cloud.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.580
Why the emphasis
specifically on ML?

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:48.600
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:52.080
And I'll definitely let
Peter jump in here too.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:54.030
From a Launchpad
perspective, there's

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:56.057
actually another
phenomenal program

00:21:56.057 --> 00:21:58.140
that's being run out of
Launchpad called Launchpad

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.620
Accelerator that's been
running for a few years now,

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:02.820
been extremely successful.

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:05.350
And what they focus on is
supporting growth stage

00:22:05.350 --> 00:22:07.050
startups in emerging markets.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.000
And the whole vision
of Launchpad--

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:10.830
so we're affiliated
with Ecosystem--

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.580
which our whole vision and
mission is to support startup

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.350
ecosystems everywhere
in the world--

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:19.530
is to identify startup
ecosystems that we think

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.200
are particularly
under-supported.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:24.930
And so with Accelerator,
it was thinking

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:26.730
about next billion users.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:29.040
What could we be doing
with startups globally

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:31.530
that would actually give
us insights and really make

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:32.580
an impact?

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.460
And then with Studio, again,
the thought process was similar.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.010
We have this, as
Peter was saying,

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:41.850
this revolution going on-- this
boom of more and more startups

00:22:41.850 --> 00:22:46.440
that are starting to for better
or for worse use AI machine

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:49.380
learning in certain cases
not in a very real way.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.900
But we identified a need and
also an opportunity for Google

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:57.480
to really make a
difference here--

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:01.140
to, as Peter said, use some of
our expertise of building some

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:03.540
of these products
internally and actually see

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:05.790
if we can teach anything
and learn anything

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:08.430
from these companies that
are going really deep looking

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:11.850
at specific use cases and
trying to scale and productize

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:13.800
some of the these things
we've been working on

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.139
for a few years.

00:23:15.139 --> 00:23:15.930
PETER NORVIG: Yeah.

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:17.940
I think at Google,
we have the luxury

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:21.270
of in some ways being able
to see into the future.

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:25.620
We're at such a scale that we
run into some of the problems

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:29.340
and opportunities
before other people who

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:30.520
are at a smaller scale.

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:32.061
So we've been doing
this for a while,

00:23:32.061 --> 00:23:34.080
and we've seen
these opportunities.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.970
And now we see a
way to share that

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:40.800
to help other companies overcome
what we've done in the past

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.474
that they're just
starting to face now.

00:23:42.474 --> 00:23:44.640
MELANIE WARRICK: You were
saying before that there's

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:46.050
mentorship that's provided.

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.567
It's a six month program.

00:23:47.567 --> 00:23:49.900
Is it similar to some of the
other incubators out there,

00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:50.730
or is it different?

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.190
Is there certain things that
make it similar or different?

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:55.481
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah, so I
think as I mentioned earlier,

00:23:55.481 --> 00:23:57.960
the main difference is that
we don't actually invest.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.930
We don't have a financial
relationship with the startups

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:02.130
that we work with.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:04.350
There's various
reasons why that is

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:07.980
the case, one of them
being that actually it

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:10.770
would be very complicated
for Google to have

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:13.020
a financial relationship
with some of the startups

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:16.020
that we're bringing in from
different places in the world.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:19.460
You mentioned China earlier.

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:21.300
We're very excited
to be able to work

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:25.719
with companies without
having some of that red tape.

00:24:25.719 --> 00:24:28.260
But it's also because we want
to make it very clear that this

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.960
is not a strategic
endeavor for Google,

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:35.310
and that this is
almost a room of peers.

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:38.490
We have a lot to learn from you.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:41.340
In many cases, we have
mentors from our product teams

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.490
at Google who come
to us and tell us

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:45.240
how much they've learned
from the startups

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:46.740
that they've been working with.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:48.490
So that's one main difference.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:50.550
The other one is,
as I also mentioned,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:52.620
we admit companies
on a rolling basis,

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:56.040
so we don't really believe
in the concept of classes.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.770
From my background working
with startups-- and Peter has

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:00.510
too-- a lot of
people on our team

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.190
were very conscientious
of the fact

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:06.670
that startups can't just
drop everything every once

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:08.680
in a while and say,
OK, we're going

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:13.180
to go in and join this program
and basically not focus

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:15.610
on product development
for however long.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:17.380
And so the entire
program is designed

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:20.110
in a way that's trying to
be very mindful of that

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:23.452
and is saying, look, you're
closing your round right now.

00:25:23.452 --> 00:25:25.660
It might not make sense for
you to join this program,

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:28.270
but in a month when we
onboard our next batch,

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:30.730
we'd love for you to come in.

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.530
So that's the other one.

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:35.530
The third aspect I'd say that
is a little bit different

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:39.600
is that we have no size fits
all-- no one size fits all.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:42.580
So it's completely
tailored to each company.

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:45.550
We admit companies
that have 12 people

00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:48.640
and have raised
$100,000 and companies

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:51.340
that have about 1,000 people
and raised $70 million.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.290
So that's kind of the
spread that we already

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:55.590
have in our current batch.

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:57.340
And so you can imagine
that a company that

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.530
has 12 people and
a company has 1,000

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:01.420
needs very different
kinds of support.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:04.704
So we actually really
focus on identifying

00:26:04.704 --> 00:26:06.370
what are the challenges
of each company,

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:12.010
and then build a totally custom
mentorship program and solution

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.470
for each company, which is
one of the reasons we only

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:17.180
work with very few
companies at a time.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:18.847
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:26:18.847 --> 00:26:20.680
Both of you talked about
that feedback loop,

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:21.650
which sounds fantastic.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:23.066
And I think that
sounds wonderful.

00:26:23.066 --> 00:26:24.730
I'm super curious to know--

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:28.997
what's been the most interesting
or surprising feedback

00:26:28.997 --> 00:26:31.330
that you've got or learning
that you've had from working

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.627
with these companies?

00:26:33.627 --> 00:26:35.460
MALIKA CANTOR: Peter,
do you have any so far

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:36.501
that you'd like to share?

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:41.460
PETER NORVIG: I think it's
just that companies are really

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:46.690
focused on solving their problem
and serving their customers,

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:51.970
and so they see the technology
as a way towards that goal.

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:56.070
And the technology itself,
exactly what it is--

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:59.590
they don't care as long
as you can get there.

00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:03.500
And so making it easier for
them to take advantage of that

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:05.832
is what we're trying to do.

00:27:05.832 --> 00:27:08.040
MELANIE WARRICK: So one of
the questions that I had--

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.206
you both were saying this,
and I've heard this a lot

00:27:10.206 --> 00:27:12.570
lately, where people will
interchange AI and ML.

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:14.400
How do you define it?

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.460
How do you think about it in
regards to especially working

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:20.100
with these startups?

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:22.740
PETER NORVIG: So I guess I
define artificial intelligence

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:25.133
as doing something smart--

00:27:25.133 --> 00:27:29.430
so figuring out what you want
to do and doing it well in a way

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:32.710
that you didn't know
how to do before.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:38.520
So in some ways, I say that
regular software is defining

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:41.760
a program to do something
that you know how to do,

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:46.570
and AI is doing it for something
that you don't know how to do.

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.110
And then machine
learning just means

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:52.830
you're going to do that
by learning from examples

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.710
rather than having the
programmer write down

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.690
some rules or something.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:03.570
And back in the 1980s,
we had the term AI,

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:05.940
and we also had the
term expert systems.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:10.440
And what that meant is you
got an expert in medicine

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:12.480
or whatever it was, and
you interviewed them,

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:13.938
and you figured
out what they knew.

00:28:13.938 --> 00:28:16.830
And then you wrote
down rules that

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:19.380
represented what they know.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:23.640
And so that was AI by
applying expert knowledge--

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:25.470
figuring it out by hand.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:26.760
Now, we tend not to do that.

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:28.800
We find that that's
brittle and slow.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:31.320
And instead, we do it by
collecting data and learning

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:32.100
from the data.

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:36.390
So machine learning is achieving
AI through applying examples,

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:41.070
and expert systems is achieving
AI through understanding

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:42.240
knowledge.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:43.320
MELANIE WARRICK: And we also
were talking a little bit

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:43.680
before.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:44.940
I just wanted to
touch on it briefly

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.150
before we start to wrap up.

00:28:46.150 --> 00:28:49.110
But you've got this great
experience working in China

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:52.310
and seeing where their world
is going around the AI space.

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:56.430
So just as a general thought
of where AI is leading us

00:28:56.430 --> 00:29:00.390
for real world
business applications,

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:03.577
what's your perception
of this ecosystem?

00:29:03.577 --> 00:29:04.410
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:07.420
I think it was extremely
exciting to see Fei-Fei

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:11.820
open the AI Research Institute
in Beijing mid-December

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:13.020
last year.

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:16.200
PETER NORVIG: A lot of
interest and excitement

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:17.770
and a lot of people
working on it.

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:20.940
And we're seeing new
things coming up.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.730
MARK MANDEL: So for anyone
who's listening and is all like,

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:25.500
I've got a business,
and we're doing ML,

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.030
and this program sounds amazing.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:28.500
How do they get involved?

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:29.269
Is there a way?

00:29:29.269 --> 00:29:30.060
MALIKA CANTOR: Yes.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:32.460
So just go on our website.

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.240
There's an application.

00:29:34.240 --> 00:29:37.440
It only takes about 10
minutes to fill out.

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:41.790
We would love to get an
application from you.

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:44.532
You're actually allowed to
apply even if you're not

00:29:44.532 --> 00:29:45.990
working on health
care and biotech,

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:50.490
because we're looking at
applications to also influence

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:53.160
the next industry verticals
that we'll be announcing.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:58.050
So again, we're admitting
companies on a rolling basis.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:01.630
Applications are open
across industry verticals,

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:04.190
but we will be working mostly
with companies in the health

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:07.530
care and biotech space
in the next few months.

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:09.960
Also, if you aren't
a startup but would

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:13.800
like to refer a company,
please reach out to me.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:15.810
And maybe we can even
share my information.

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:16.470
MELANIE WARRICK:
Yes, we'll definitely

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:18.090
have them in the show notes.

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:19.715
MALIKA CANTOR: And
then the final thing

00:30:19.715 --> 00:30:22.380
is also if you are a subject
matter expert, because I think

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:24.480
one of the big
themes in this talk

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:28.260
was basically effectively
moving AI machine learning out

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:30.870
of the hands of just AI experts
and moving them increasingly

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.200
into the hands of subject
matter experts like health

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:34.680
care and biotech experts.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:36.900
If you are a health
care and biotech expert

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.390
or someone else in
another industry,

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:42.090
you're really passionate
about applying

00:30:42.090 --> 00:30:44.000
machine learning and
AI to your industry

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:45.680
and you'd like to
become a mentor,

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:47.080
again, please reach out.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:50.940
We work with academics,
with industry veterans,

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:53.480
with ex-CEOs.

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:59.155
We'd love to hear about your
experience and your expertise.

00:30:59.155 --> 00:31:00.990
MELANIE WARRICK:
Nice that you give it

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:04.894
such a breadth in terms of being
willing to take in the mentors.

00:31:04.894 --> 00:31:06.810
One of the things I know
we were talking about

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.820
before was that you guys
have an event coming up

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:09.722
in a couple weeks.

00:31:09.722 --> 00:31:11.680
Do you want to tell us
a little bit about that?

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:12.513
MALIKA CANTOR: Sure.

00:31:12.513 --> 00:31:15.890
So basically, it's our
second high touchpoint.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:19.470
So that's what we call the event
when the startups basically

00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:20.280
fly in.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:21.510
In certain cases, we
have two companies

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:23.885
that are based outside the US
and two companies in the US

00:31:23.885 --> 00:31:26.460
right now, and they
come and basically

00:31:26.460 --> 00:31:31.650
get few days to a week of back
to back workshops and meetings

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:33.852
with a lot of Google
teams and external mentors

00:31:33.852 --> 00:31:34.560
like I mentioned.

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:37.790
So our next high
touchpoint is coming up.

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:41.810
We're going to be onboarding
a few new companies.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.090
So if you're interested
in seeing what

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.920
other types of use
cases and companies

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:47.753
we're going to be
supporting moving forward,

00:31:47.753 --> 00:31:49.580
definitely keep an
eye on our website

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:53.390
and on our Google
Developers blog.

00:31:53.390 --> 00:31:56.030
And yeah, we're
really excited to keep

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:58.580
working with these companies,
and as we mentioned,

00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:00.980
develop product methodologies
for machine learning

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:03.525
and hopefully build better
tools for this whole ecosystem.

00:32:03.525 --> 00:32:04.650
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.941
Is there anything else that
you both wanted to touch on

00:32:06.941 --> 00:32:08.090
before we close out?

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:10.490
This is really great to
hear about Launchpad Studio

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:12.710
and get some insights
in terms of how

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:14.007
you're supporting startups.

00:32:14.007 --> 00:32:14.840
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah.

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:17.810
I guess the only thing is if
Peter has any other thoughts,

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:21.050
because him and I have had a lot
of conversations about this--

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:25.340
about his interest and
passion, and again, basically

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:28.580
empowering people
who aren't AI experts

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:30.650
across different industries.

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:33.470
PETER NORVIG: I guess we really
enjoy working with companies

00:32:33.470 --> 00:32:35.570
and understanding
what their needs are

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:39.947
and what kind of tools
they want and how

00:32:39.947 --> 00:32:41.030
we're going to build that.

00:32:41.030 --> 00:32:45.550
And we see that as real
challenges going forward.

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:47.630
So I think a good
example of that

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:50.240
is with speech
recognition, where

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:53.240
we've made great strides
in the last few years--

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:56.150
where now you can
talk to your phone,

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:58.800
and we're going to get
most of the words right.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:02.120
But now there's a next big step
of you got all the words right,

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:06.030
but how do you respond
to those words?

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.420
And that doesn't have to
do just with language,

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:11.360
that has to do with what is it
that you're trying to achieve.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:14.360
And so we've got to give
the companies the capability

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:17.120
to say, when I
recognize these words,

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:20.760
here's what I should
do, and do that in a way

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:23.570
that they can
understand and build

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:26.900
a good product without having
to be experts in speech

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:30.110
recognition and machine learning
and linguistics and everything

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:33.010
else but just integrated
into their product.

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:37.490
And again, it's like in
regular software engineering.

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:41.210
We figured out how to build
menus and buttons and mouse

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:44.330
clicks over decades,
and now we have

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:47.300
to do the same thing
with a speech interface.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.430
And it's all going
to be new, and we

00:33:49.430 --> 00:33:50.600
don't know how to do it yet.

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:53.600
So we're looking to
partner with companies

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:55.552
and help figure
it out the other.

00:33:55.552 --> 00:33:57.593
MALIKA CANTOR: Yeah, so
I think to Peter's point,

00:33:57.593 --> 00:34:00.770
it's almost like come and
help us learn, and teach us

00:34:00.770 --> 00:34:01.920
what you need.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.930
And we're really excited about
working with a lot of people.

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:06.897
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:34:06.897 --> 00:34:08.480
And actually, just
one other question.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:11.389
Peter, what got you inspired
to do AI in general?

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:14.409
What got you into the field,
if I may ask this slight side

00:34:14.409 --> 00:34:16.204
note?

00:34:16.204 --> 00:34:18.620
PETER NORVIG: It just seemed
like a really exciting field.

00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:22.760
And I was in high school,
and I was lucky enough

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.520
that my high school
had both a computer

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:27.150
class and a linguistics class.

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:31.790
So it was rare that you had
computers in high school

00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:33.420
in those days.

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:36.000
And I took those two
classes and said, hey,

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.030
could we put these two together?

00:34:38.030 --> 00:34:40.151
And my teacher
wisely said, yeah,

00:34:40.151 --> 00:34:41.900
it's possible, but not
with what you know.

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:44.600
So ever since, I've
been trying to learn

00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:45.725
enough to be able to do it.

00:34:45.725 --> 00:34:47.683
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
great that she gave you

00:34:47.683 --> 00:34:50.120
that kind of challenge and
that you took the challenge.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.756
I'm glad you both
were able to join us.

00:34:51.756 --> 00:34:53.630
Thank you so much for
sharing the information

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:55.370
around Launchpad Studios.

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.710
And anything else that
you wanted to mention

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:00.622
or anything else that you wanted
to plug before we wrap this up?

00:35:00.622 --> 00:35:01.580
MARK MANDEL: All right.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:02.870
Well, thank you
very much to both

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:05.160
of you for taking the time
today and having a chat with us.

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:06.270
MALIKA CANTOR: Thank you
so much for having us.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:07.010
PETER NORVIG: Yeah, thanks.

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:08.180
MELANIE WARRICK:
Thanks Malika and Peter

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:09.500
for that great interview.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:12.110
We appreciated the insights
around Launchpad Studio,

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:13.670
especially for
those startups out

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:15.336
there that are machine
learning and want

00:35:15.336 --> 00:35:18.842
to have access to different
tooling and resources.

00:35:18.842 --> 00:35:21.050
If you have any questions
or want additional insights

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:22.716
on Launchpad Studio,
we will, as always,

00:35:22.716 --> 00:35:24.680
put links on our website.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:27.170
And now, let's talk about
the question of the week.

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:30.800
So mark, if I am a startup, and
I want credits to work on GCP,

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:31.850
what is available to me?

00:35:31.850 --> 00:35:32.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:35:32.600 --> 00:35:34.520
So we have a whole
startups program.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:36.906
We may have talked about it
previously on the podcast,

00:35:36.906 --> 00:35:38.780
but I think it's changed
a little since then,

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:39.696
which is kind of cool.

00:35:39.696 --> 00:35:42.050
So if you're a startup
and you're like, oh, man,

00:35:42.050 --> 00:35:44.240
I really want to use
Google Cloud for it,

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:47.090
you can go to
cloud.google.com/startups.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:49.424
And it will show you the
startup page, which is here.

00:35:49.424 --> 00:35:50.840
And there are
different tiers that

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:53.890
are available right now for
different types of startup

00:35:53.890 --> 00:35:54.390
packages.

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:57.680
So you can get anywhere from
$3,000 in credit all the way up

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.970
to $100,000 in
credit for one year,

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:03.496
including as well 24/7
support, office hours,

00:36:03.496 --> 00:36:05.120
Spotlight all sorts
of different things

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:06.828
depending on what tier
you're on and what

00:36:06.828 --> 00:36:07.910
sort of needs you have.

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:10.662
There are several-- actually,
we've had Descarte Labs--

00:36:10.662 --> 00:36:12.620
which have been on the
podcast more than once--

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:14.720
is one of those people who
came through that program.

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:15.920
The one thing to know though--

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:17.540
if you are a startup
and you're looking,

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:18.990
and you want to come
through this program,

00:36:18.990 --> 00:36:21.320
you need to apply through
your incubator, accelerator,

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:23.060
or VC firm.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:25.220
There's a long list
of all those people

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:27.015
that we work with--
our platform partners.

00:36:27.015 --> 00:36:29.180
I think I counted 142.

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:30.130
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:32.402
That's good number of
people to be working with.

00:36:32.402 --> 00:36:33.860
So yeah, if you
are small business,

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:35.359
and you're looking
for some credits,

00:36:35.359 --> 00:36:38.427
it gives some insights in terms
of the size, what that means,

00:36:38.427 --> 00:36:40.010
a little bit of
information around who

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:42.170
would qualify for the program.

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:43.350
Check out our link.

00:36:43.350 --> 00:36:45.260
You'll see that
there's Start package,

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:46.760
there's a Spark
package, and there's

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:48.801
a Surge package in terms
of the number of credits

00:36:48.801 --> 00:36:50.540
that you can
potentially get hold of.

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:53.342
And this can be applied to
all of GCP and Firebase.

00:36:53.342 --> 00:36:54.050
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:36:54.050 --> 00:36:55.550
Actually, that's a
really good point

00:36:55.550 --> 00:36:57.440
that it goes to both
GCP and Firebase,

00:36:57.440 --> 00:36:58.820
which is also super cool.

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:01.145
If you aren't part of
one of those groups--

00:37:01.145 --> 00:37:03.020
those incubators or
accelerator programs--

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:04.850
there is a mailing list
you can sign up for just

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:06.641
to get updates-- to
see if anything changes

00:37:06.641 --> 00:37:08.440
or if new people come on board.

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:10.070
That's there, and
obviously free trial

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:11.322
is also obviously available.

00:37:11.322 --> 00:37:12.530
MELANIE WARRICK: There we go.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:15.230
Well, that wraps us up for
at least our first interview

00:37:15.230 --> 00:37:16.730
out the gate for 2018.

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:18.805
Anything you want to
actually share or any

00:37:18.805 --> 00:37:20.930
